For $a,b\in\mathcal A$, subtract the weak equations to obtain $A_{1+a}(u_{1+a,\ell k}-u_{1+b,\ell k},v)=-\int(a-b)
abla u_{1+b,\ell k}\cdot\overline{
abla v}$. By step 1.1 and the summed interior bound in step 3.1, the sequence of differences has $\ell^2(H^1)$ norm at most $C\|a-b\|_\infty$. For $h\in E$, the same estimate constructs the asserted derivative sequence $v_{a,h}$ with norm at most $C\|h\|_\infty$. Subtracting its equation from the difference equation for $a+h,a$ shows that the remainder has forcing $-h
abla(u_{1+a+h}-u_{1+a})$ and hence $\ell^2(H^1)$ norm at most $C\|h\|_\infty^2$. Taking traces proves Fréchet differentiability. Finally, subtract the derivative equations at $a,b$: $A_{1+a}(v_{a,h}-v_{b,h},w)=-\int h
abla(u_{1+a}-u_{1+b})\cdot\overline{
abla w}-\int(a-b)
abla v_{b,h}\cdot\overline{
abla w}$. The established estimates bound its sequence norm by $C\|a-b\|_\infty\|h\|_\infty$, proving the operator-norm Lipschitz derivative bound and the asserted Lipschitz bound for $G$.

Multiply that identity by a nonnegative Lipschitz weight $\eta$ and integrate over $M$. In each chart the product of $\eta$ with a smooth coefficient is Lipschitz, so [F4] gives its weak derivative by difference quotients. Choose a boundary-compatible coordinate cover and subordinate partition $(\chi_j)$ by [F7]. On a boundary half-chart, one-dimensional integration by parts in the normal half-line includes the endpoint value; [F6] identifies its coordinate density with $\eta\operatorname{Re}(\overline{\partial_tu}\,\partial_{
u_{out}}u)\,dS_g$. Summing the partitioned fields $\chi_j\eta\overline{\partial_tu}
abla_gu$ cancels the extra terms since $\sum_j
abla_g\chi_j=0$. For $E_\eta(t)=\int_M\eta e\,dV_g$, this yields $$E_\eta'(t)=\int_M ig((\partial_t\eta)e-\operatorname{Re}(\overline{\partial_tu}\,g(
abla_g\eta,
abla_gu))+\eta\operatorname{Re}((1-q)\overline u\,\partial_tu) ig)\,dV_g+\int_{\partial M}\eta\operatorname{Re}(\overline{\partial_tu}\,\partial_{
u_{out}}u)\,dS_g.$$ The identity holds for each fixed $t$; the spatial weak-derivative calculation applies at every such time, while $\eta,\partial_t\eta,e$ are bounded and continuous in $(t,x)$. Differentiation under the integral makes $E_\eta$ continuously differentiable.
